[# Section 5 — analytics, columnar formats, and bridges.
   Authored 2026-05-22; repaired 2026-08-20 against the verification
   audit (real table syntax, real binding surfaces, wire facts).
   Anchored in spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md +
   spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md §3.10-3.13 +
   spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md.
   Cross-links use [[anchor]] form. #]

[section id=analytics group='Ring 0 — Data' n=5 title='Analytics, columnar formats, and bridges'

  [child id=overview n=5.1 title='The analytics lane'
    [intro
      """CX has an analytics lane. The same brackets that carry a config file
         also carry a typed columnar table; the same canonical-bytes-plus-hash
         identity that anchors a single document also anchors a billion-row
         dataset; the same evaluator that templates a Markdown report also
         streams over a multi-gigabyte file in bounded memory. The lane has
         three load-bearing pieces, and this section is about how they
         compose."""]

    [body
      """The three pieces are: a **logical data shape** (the `[table[…]]`
         block, with typed columns and nullable cells); a **physical wire
         format** (CXCol, the column-oriented streaming binary form,
         tag `0x63` in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.11);
         and **bridges to the broader ecosystem** (Apache Arrow C Data
         Interface for zero-copy interop, Apache Parquet for cross-tool
         storage)."""]

    [list
      [item """**Logical**: `[orders [table[id::int customer::string]] …]`
              — see [[table-shape]]. Authored in CX text, parses into the
              same AST as any other CX element, validates against `.cxs`
              schemas, hashes via SHA-256 over canonical bytes."""]
      [item """**Physical**: CXCol — the column-oriented streaming
              binary form. Row-group chunked, optionally zstd-compressed
              per chunk, optionally schema-driven for tag-density.
              See [[cxcol]]."""]
      [item """**Bridges**: Arrow C Data Interface (zero-copy in-process
              handoff) and Parquet (cross-ecosystem storage). See
              [[arrow-bridge]] and [[parquet-bridge]]."""]
      [item """**Streaming**: the CX streaming evaluator (see
              [`spec/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md)) emits
              `StartTable` / `RowGroup` / `EndTable` events over CXCol
              data, so directives like `[?for]` and `[?modify]` can
              operate on tables that don't fit in memory."""]
      [item """**Identity**: CXCol files have a stable SHA-256 over their
              canonical (uncompressed) bytes. Compression level does not
              change the hash. Chunk boundaries do — see
              [[cxcol-identity]] for the rule."""]]

    [body
      """The rest of this section walks each piece. The normative spec
         documents are [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10-3.13 (wire format) and
         [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)
         (host binding API). This guide is the working developer
         summary."""]

    [note
      """**Naming.** `CXCol` is the column-oriented streaming format
         name. The `CXDB` / `CXDatabase` namespace is reserved for a
         future query-engine product and does not refer to the file
         format. Throughout this guide, CXCol is the format; `.cxbin` is
         the file extension (`.cxcol` is a deprecated alias, recognized
         read-only — [`spec/process/governance.md`](../../spec/03-approved/process/governance.md)
         §12.2); the `cx_table_*` prefix appears on the C ABI streaming
         identifiers ([`spec/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.10)."""]]

  [child id=table-shape n=5.2 title='The [table[…]] data shape'
    [intro
      """The `[table[…]]` clause-child opener declares a typed columnar
         table at the logical CX layer. It is a CX element like any
         other — same brackets, same parse rules, same canonical-bytes
         contract — but the parser recognizes the glued `[table[` opener
         (grammar [29]) and produces a `Table` value rather than a
         generic element. The element's own name is the table's name."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[orders [table[id::int customer::string amount::float ts::datetime]]
           1 Ada   42.50 2026-05-22T10:00:00Z
           2 Grace 17.25 2026-05-22T10:15:00Z
           3 Linus 99.00 2026-05-22T11:30:00Z]"""]

    [body
      """The `[table[…]]` clause carries the column spec: whitespace-
         separated `name::type` entries, where an entry without a type
         (`[table[a b c]]`) declares a string column. The body is a flat
         run of whitespace-separated cells, partitioned into rows by the
         column count (grammar [29c] — each row supplies exactly one
         cell per column). A cell that cannot carry the declared column
         type refuses loudly rather than coercing — the encode-refusal
         rule of [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10.3 (never wrap, never widen, never approximate)."""]

    [body
      """Supported column types match the CX scalar type system:
         `int` and the sized integers (`i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`),
         `bigint`, `float` and the reduced widths (`f32`, `f16`),
         `decimal`, `bool`, `string`, `bytes`, `date`, and `datetime`.
         The full type catalog with byte encodings is in
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10.3 + [`spec/misc/type-mapping.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/type-mapping.md)."""]

    [child id=nullable n=5.2.1 title='Null cells'
      [body
        """Any column can carry null cells. A null cell is written as
           the literal `null` — a bare token in cell position; empty
           cells are not allowed in the wire form."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[customers [table[id::int name::string phone::string]]
             1 Ada   '555-1212'
             2 Grace null
             3 Linus '555-9999']"""]
      [body
        """On the wire, a column with null cells uses the nullable
           wrapper of [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.10.5: type code `0x80`, then the inner type code, then a
           null bitmap of `ceil(rows/8)` bytes (bit set = null), then
           the packed payload of the non-null cells only. The host
           binding's `Table.cell(r, c)` returns the host's null sentinel
           (`None` in Python, `nil` in Go, `Option::None` in Rust)."""]]

    [child id=dict-encoding n=5.2.2 title='Dictionary encoding'
      [body
        """Dictionary encoding is an **opt-in container form** — tag
           `0x62` in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.10.2. Each column's payload starts with a marker: plain
           (the §3.10.3 layout) or dictionary-encoded (distinct values
           in insertion order, then one varint index per row). Decoders
           rebuild the values transparently; the host-binding API never
           exposes the indices."""]
      [body
        """Canonical writers emit the plain `0x60` form. A writer that
           opts into `0x62` uses a dictionary for a column only when the
           dictionary actually saves bytes — the canonical constraint of
           §3.10.2, evaluated per column — so categorical data (status,
           region, type) dictionary-encodes and high-cardinality data
           stays plain. CXPath `=` and `[?match]` value comparison
           operate on the decoded value, so user code is
           dictionary-agnostic."""]]

    [child id=table-validate n=5.2.3 title='Schema validation'
      [body
        """Tables validate against `.cxs` schemas the same way ordinary
           elements do — the table block is an element, and the schema
           language of [`spec/core/schema.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)
           applies to it as to any other element. See [[data#schema]]
           for the schema language. At the binary layer, a CXCol file
           can carry a schema reference that names its schema by
           content hash (see [[cxcol-schema-driven]])."""]]

    [child id=table-api n=5.2.4 title='The Table host-binding API'
      [intro
        """Bindings expose a `Table` value type for `[table[…]]`
           blocks. Methods match across bindings up to host-idiom
           differences; the canonical surface is documented in
           [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           t = cxlib.Table.from_cx(open('orders.cx').read())

           print(t.cols)         # ['id', 'customer', 'amount', 'ts']
           print(t.types)        # ['int', 'string', 'float', 'datetime']
           print(t.row_count)    # 3
           print(t.row(0))       # {'id': 1, 'customer': 'Ada', ...}
           print(t.column('amount'))  # [42.5, 17.25, 99.0]

           # Slicing / projection
           sub = t.head(2).select_cols(['customer', 'amount'])
           print(sub.to_cx())"""]
      [body
        """The canonical surface
           ([`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md) §3)
           is: the `cols` / `types` / `row_count` / `col_count`
           properties; the `row` / `column` / `col_at` / `cell` /
           `cell_by_name` accessors; the `slice` / `head` / `tail` /
           `select_cols` projections; row iteration; and the `to_cx` /
           `to_csv` / `to_json` / `to_data_bin` / `to_dict_list`
           conversions. The same shape exists in V, Python, Go, and
           Rust with the naming conventions of each host."""]]]

  [child id=cxcol n=5.3 title='CXCol — column-oriented streaming format'
    [intro
      """CXCol is the physical wire form for tabular data: column-oriented,
         row-group-chunked, content-addressable, and streamable in bounded
         memory. The data-bin tag is `0x63`; the file extension is
         `.cxbin`. The normative byte-level layout is in
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.11."""]

    [body
      """The format is a single sequential pass: header, column spec,
         then a stream of row groups, then a terminator. Writers emit one
         row group at a time and can produce a billion-row table in
         memory bounded by the largest row group. Readers pull row groups
         one at a time and can skip groups by their length prefix without
         decoding them."""]

    [child id=cxcol-layout n=5.3.1 title='Wire layout'
      [body
        """A CXCol stream is:"""]
      [list
        [item """**12-byte header.** Magic `CXCol` (5 bytes,
                `0x43 0x58 0x43 0x6F 0x6C`), version byte (`0x01`),
                flags byte, `max_depth` (uint32 LE), reserved byte.
                Flag bit 0 is endianness (1 = little-endian); flag
                bit 1 signals schema-driven mode (see
                [[cxcol-schema-driven]]); bits 2–7 are reserved and
                must be zero."""]
        [item """**Column spec.** The chunked-table tag `0x63`, then
                `uvarint(col_count)`, then one col-spec per column —
                the column name as a tagged string plus its type code
                (the §3.10.1 shape). Declared once at the head — every
                row group in the file conforms to this spec."""]
        [item """**Row-group sequence.** Each group is
                `uvarint(body_byte_len) <body-tag> <body>`. `body-tag`
                `0x01` = plain (`uvarint(row_count)` then column-major
                buffers); `0x90` = the zstd page-compression wrapper
                (§3.12), whose decompressed bytes form the same plain
                body."""]
        [item """**Terminator.** A single `0x00` byte (a zero-length
                row group) closes the stream."""]]
      [body
        """The 2²⁰ (1,048,576) rows per group is the canonical chunk
           size — `cx canonical` and `cx hash` re-chunk a CXCol input
           to it, and writers targeting cross-system hash invariance
           should use it. Different chunk sizes produce valid CXCol
           files but with different SHA-256 hashes."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-identity n=5.3.2 title='Content-addressable identity'
      [body
        """A CXCol file's SHA-256 hash is computed over its **canonical
           uncompressed bytes** — the bytes you would see if every
           `0x90` page-compression wrapper were unwrapped to its `0x01`
           plain form. Compression is a transport / storage choice;
           identity is a content choice; the format keeps them
           orthogonal."""]
      [list
        [item """**Same data + same chunking + same schema → same hash**,
                regardless of zstd level or whether compression was
                applied at all."""]
        [item """**Different chunk size → different hash.** Chunk
                boundaries are part of the canonical form. The
                2²⁰-rows-per-group convention is the cross-writer
                interoperability rule."""]
        [item """**Hash composes with [[hash]].** The same SHA-256
                primitive that hashes a CX document hashes a CXCol file —
                tooling pipelines (caching, dedup, signing,
                content-addressable storage) treat both uniformly."""]]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           # Framed CXCol chunked-table bytes: [u32 LE size][CXCol payload]
           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())
           # The payload's identity is the SHA-256 of its canonical
           # (uncompressed) bytes: a 0x90-compressed row group unwraps
           # to its 0x01 plain form before hashing, so the compression
           # level never moves the hash."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-streaming-api n=5.3.3 title='Streaming reader / writer'
      [intro
        """The streaming surface is the C ABI reader / writer family in
           [`spec/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.10
           (capability bit 21): `cx_table_reader_open` /
           `cx_table_reader_open_fd` / `cx_table_reader_schema` /
           `cx_table_reader_next` / `cx_table_reader_close`, and
           `cx_table_writer_open` / `cx_table_writer_open_fd` /
           `cx_table_writer_emit_row_group` /
           `cx_table_writer_close_get_bytes` / `cx_table_writer_close`.
           The Python binding wraps the family as `cxlib.TableReader` /
           `cxlib.TableWriter`."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('big.cx').read())

           # Reader — pulls one row group at a time
           with cxlib.TableReader(framed) as r:
               spec = r.schema()          # col-spec payload bytes
               for group in r:            # row-group payloads, one at a time
                   process(group)

           # Writer — pushes one row group at a time
           with cxlib.TableWriter(spec) as w:
               for group in source_of_groups():
                   w.emit(group)          # bounded memory per group
               framed_out = w.close_get_bytes()"""]
      [body
        """Go and Rust reach the same reader / writer handles through
           the shared C ABI directly — the §2.10 symbol family is the
           contract; a packaged native wrapper in those bindings has
           not shipped yet."""]
      [body
        """Memory budget is one row group in flight per stage in the
           pipeline. A reader chained into a CXPath filter chained into a
           Parquet writer holds three row groups at once, total —
           regardless of the table's logical row count. See
           [[streaming]] for the directive-level view."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-schema-driven n=5.3.4 title='Schema-driven mode'
      [body
        """When the writer knows both sides of the wire have access to
           the schema, header flag bit 1 enables schema-driven mode: per-
           value type tags are omitted for values the schema declares;
           reader and writer walk the schema in lockstep with the data.
           Wire density approaches Parquet's typed encoding."""]
      [body
        """Schema-driven mode is opt-in — `cx_to_data_bin
           --schema-driven` at the ABI, `cxlib.to_data_bin_schema_driven(…)`
           in Python; having a schema in scope does not silently promote
           the encoder. The schema reference forms (`0x10` content-hash,
           `0x11` inline, `0x12` content-hash + name hint, `0x13`
           self-describing multihash) are documented in
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.13.1;
           the `cx table` subcommand resolves `--schema-from=` arguments
           to a content hash before emit."""]
      [note
        """Schema-driven mode requires the reader to retrieve the schema
           by its content hash. Local lookup is the default; the
           callback-shaped content-store ABI is a planned
           follow-up."""]]]

  [child id=arrow-bridge n=5.4 title='Arrow bridge — zero-copy interop'
    [intro
      """CXCol bridges to Apache Arrow via the Arrow **C Data Interface**
         — a struct-based ABI that lets two libraries share columnar
         buffers without copying them. The CX side is `libcx_arrow`, a
         separate dynamic library that depends on libcx + the Arrow
         C-Data ABI. Core libcx is Arrow-free; capability bit 23
         advertises Arrow when libcx_arrow is loaded."""]

    [body
      """The bridge exports four C ABI symbols
         ([`spec/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.11):
         `cx_arrow_export_open` / `cx_arrow_export_open_fd` for
         CXCol → Arrow, and `cx_arrow_import_to_data_bin` /
         `cx_arrow_import_to_data_bin_fd` for Arrow → CXCol. The Python
         binding wraps them as `cxlib.arrow.export` (framed CXCol bytes
         in, `pyarrow.RecordBatchReader` out) and
         `cxlib.arrow.import_to_data_bin` (the inverse)."""]

    [child id=arrow-zero-copy n=5.4.1 title='Zero-copy handoff'
      [body
        """The Arrow C Data Interface uses two structs: `ArrowSchema`
           (column types + names) and `ArrowArray` (per-batch buffers,
           including null bitmap, offsets, and values). CX populates
           these structs to point at its own column buffers; consumers
           (DuckDB, Polars, PyArrow, etc.) read from the same memory
           pages CX wrote."""]
      [body
        """Ownership crosses the boundary explicitly. The exporter
           populates an `ArrowArrayStream` with a `release` callback;
           the consumer calls `release` when done. CX's release callback
           frees the underlying CXCol buffers. There is no GC interplay
           — bindings hold the Arrow stream as an opaque handle until
           they're done with it."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.arrow as cxa

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())

           # Zero-copy export: pointer handoff over the C Data Interface
           reader = cxa.export(framed)       # pyarrow.RecordBatchReader
           pa_table = reader.read_all()      # pyarrow.Table

           # Symmetric inverse: Arrow → framed CXCol bytes
           framed_again = cxa.import_to_data_bin(pa_table)"""]
      [body
        """Go and Rust use the same four symbols through the C ABI with
           each host's Arrow FFI layer (`arrow-go`, the `arrow` crate's
           `ffi_stream` module) — the per-binding Arrow libraries are
           tabulated in [`spec/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md)
           §2.11."""]]

    [child id=arrow-type-mapping n=5.4.2 title='Type mapping'
      [body
        """The CX ↔ Arrow type map is normative
           ([`spec/misc/type-mapping.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/type-mapping.md)).
           The high-level summary:"""]
      [list
        [item """`int` / `i64` ↔ `Int64`; `i32` ↔ `Int32`; sized
                variants (`i8`–`u64`) map to corresponding Arrow
                sized integer types."""]
        [item """`float` / `f64` ↔ `Float64`; `f32` ↔ `Float32`;
                `f16` ↔ `Float16`."""]
        [item """`bool` ↔ `Boolean` (bit-packed in Arrow)."""]
        [item """`string` ↔ `Utf8` (or `LargeUtf8` for columns whose
                total byte length exceeds 2 GB)."""]
        [item """`bytes` ↔ `Binary` (or `LargeBinary` similarly)."""]
        [item """`date` ↔ `Date32` (days since 1970-01-01,
                proleptic Gregorian)."""]
        [item """`datetime` ↔ `Timestamp(Nanoseconds, UTC)` (Arrow
                format string `tsn:UTC`)."""]
        [item """`decimal` ↔ `Decimal128` / `Decimal256` by coefficient
                width."""]
        [item """Nullable columns → Arrow's standard nullable variant
                (validity bitmap)."""]]
      [body
        """Current column-type coverage in `libcx_arrow` is **all ten
           column types** — int / i8 / i16 / i32 / float / bool /
           string / date / bytes / datetime (the last as
           `timestamp[ns, UTC]`). Deferred: `decimal` and
           dictionary-encoded columns — each surfaces a clear
           `not yet supported` error rather than a lossy fallback."""]]

    [child id=arrow-ipc n=5.4.3 title='Arrow IPC stream format'
      [body
        """Beyond zero-copy in-process handoff, CXCol can emit Arrow IPC
           streams for cross-process or cross-host transport. The IPC
           stream is Arrow's own serialization (schema header + record
           batches + EOF), distinct from the C Data Interface
           (in-memory structs)."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.arrow as cxa

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())

           ipc = cxa.to_ipc(framed)              # Arrow IPC stream bytes
           cxa.write_ipc_file(framed, 'orders.arrow')

           framed_back = cxa.from_ipc(ipc)       # inverse
           framed_back2 = cxa.read_ipc_file('orders.arrow')"""]
      [body
        """The `cx table dump FILE --to=arrow [--output=FILE]` CLI
           subcommand ([`spec/misc/cli.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/cli.md))
           writes the IPC stream form."""]]

    [child id=arrow-flight n=5.4.4 title='Arrow Flight (planned)'
      [body
        """Arrow Flight is Arrow's gRPC-based RPC framing for cross-
           process record batches. It is the natural next layer above
           the IPC stream — a Flight server publishes batches by
           descriptor; a Flight client subscribes. CX integration is
           **planned**: no Flight surface ships currently; the
           tooling-layer story will be specified when it lands."""]
      [note
        """For cross-process CXCol transport currently, use Arrow IPC
           over your existing transport (HTTP, pipe, socket). The
           `[?http-client]` directive can carry Arrow IPC streams as
           binary bodies."""]]]

  [child id=parquet-bridge n=5.5 title='Parquet bridge'
    [intro
      """CXCol bridges to Apache Parquet via Arrow: CXCol → Arrow stream
         → Parquet file. Core libcx has **no direct Parquet C++
         dependency** — adding one would couple every binding to a
         heavyweight build-time toolchain. The Parquet adapter lives at
         the binding layer, chained off the Arrow bridge; the Python
         binding ships the reference composition (`cxlib.parquet`, on
         `pyarrow.parquet`)."""]

    [child id=parquet-write n=5.5.1 title='Writing Parquet'
      [intro
        """`cxlib.parquet.write_table(cx_data_bin, path, …)` walks
           framed CXCol bytes → Arrow record batches → the PyArrow
           Parquet writer. The Parquet writer is the host binding's
           choice; the CXCol side just provides the Arrow stream."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.parquet as cxpq

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())
           cxpq.write_table(
               framed,
               'orders.parquet',
               compression='snappy',     # Parquet ecosystem default
               row_group_size=1_048_576, # match CXCol canonical chunk
           )"""]
      [body
        """Defaults are the Parquet-ecosystem defaults (Snappy, not
           zstd) — output is meant to be consumed by Parquet readers
           that assume Snappy. Zstd is CX's default for CXCol's `0x90`
           page compression; the two formats use their own ecosystem
           defaults to minimize friction with the consumers of
           each."""]
      [body
        """[`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md) §3
           declares the per-binding adapter slots (Rust via `arrow-rs` +
           the `parquet` crate, Go via `arrow-go`); those bindings reach
           Parquet through the Arrow C ABI today — the packaged one-call
           adapters have not shipped outside Python."""]]

    [child id=parquet-read n=5.5.2 title='Reading Parquet'
      [intro
        """`cxlib.parquet.read_table(path)` is the symmetric inverse:
           the PyArrow Parquet reader produces Arrow record batches, and
           the bridge imports them as framed CXCol chunked-table
           bytes — ready for `cxlib.from_data_bin` or a streaming
           `cxlib.TableReader` pass."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.parquet as cxpq

           framed = cxpq.read_table('orders.parquet')

           # Whole-value view
           print(cxlib.from_data_bin(framed))

           # Streaming view — one row group at a time
           with cxlib.TableReader(framed) as r:
               for group in r:
                   process(group)"""]
      [body
        """Column projection and predicate pushdown are the **host
           Parquet reader's** work, done before the batches reach CX:
           a Parquet reader given per-row-group statistics skips the
           groups a predicate rules out, so I/O saved is proportional
           to the row groups skipped. Files without statistics still
           read correctly — the reader degrades to a full scan."""]]

    [child id=parquet-types n=5.5.3 title='CX ↔ Parquet type mapping'
      [body
        """The mapping is normative. Most types round-trip cleanly
           through Arrow; a small number of CX types have lossy edges,
           and the bridge reports them loudly rather than silently
           coercing."""]
      [list
        [item """`int` / `i64` → `INT64`."""]
        [item """`i32` → `INT32`; `i16` / `i8` → `INT32` with
                `INT(N, true)` logical type; unsigned similarly."""]
        [item """`f64` / `float` → `DOUBLE`; `f32` → `FLOAT`;
                `f16` → `FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY(2)` / `FLOAT16`
                (the latter is non-standard pre-Parquet 1.13)."""]
        [item """`bool` → `BOOLEAN`."""]
        [item """`string` → `BYTE_ARRAY` with `STRING` logical type
                (UTF-8)."""]
        [item """`bytes` → `BYTE_ARRAY`."""]
        [item """`date` → `INT32` with `DATE` logical type."""]
        [item """`datetime` (UTC) → `INT64` with
                `TIMESTAMP(NANOS, isAdjustedToUTC=true)`."""]
        [item """`decimal` → `DECIMAL(p, s)` (Parquet caps at
                256-bit precision)."""]
        [item """Nullable columns → `OPTIONAL` repetition."""]]
      [body
        """Types outside the bridge's current coverage (see
           [[arrow-type-mapping]]) refuse with a clear error at the
           Arrow boundary rather than degrading — the same
           loud-never-lossy posture as the wire format itself."""]]

    [child id=parquet-roundtrip n=5.5.4 title='Round-trip identity'
      [body
        """CX → Parquet → CX is not in general byte-identical. Parquet
           is a presentation-layer format: footer offsets shift,
           row-group sizes are writer-dependent, encoding choices
           (dictionary thresholds, page sizes, compression codec) are
           per-writer. Many byte-level Parquet files encode the same
           logical data. CXCol fills this niche precisely — it is the
           hashable origin; Parquet is the bridge."""]
      [body
        """**Logical** round-trip is normative: a CX table emitted as
           Parquet and re-imported produces the same `[table[…]]` block
           with the same column names, types, row count, and cell
           values (modulo the documented lossy edges in 5.5.3)."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.parquet as cxpq

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())
           cxpq.write_table(framed, 'orders.parquet')
           framed2 = cxpq.read_table('orders.parquet')

           # Logical equality holds across the round-trip
           assert cxlib.from_data_bin(framed) == cxlib.from_data_bin(framed2)"""]]

    [child id=parquet-metadata n=5.5.5 title='Schema identity across the bridge'
      [body
        """Parquet output carries the data, not the CX schema
           reference. CX schema identity lives at the CXCol layer: a
           schema-driven file names its schema by content hash (the
           §3.13.1 reference forms), and the `cx table` subcommand
           resolves `--schema-from=` arguments to a content hash before
           emit ([`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.13.1). A Parquet round-trip re-enters CX as a
           self-describing table; re-attach the schema by validating or
           re-encoding schema-driven against the same schema hash."""]]]

  [child id=streaming n=5.6 title='Streaming evaluator over columnar data'
    [intro
      """The CX streaming evaluator
         ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
         operates over CXCol chunks one at a time. The normative
         throughput gate is **≥ 200 MB/s sustained per streaming
         shape** ([`spec/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
         §11.4.4, gate 15). The gate is not green at this release:
         `[?for]` measures ~200 MB/s and `[?map]` 129.2 MB/s; closing
         the `[?map]` gap is tracked for the next cycle."""]

    [body
      """The streaming evaluator emits three table-specific events when
         it encounters a CXCol input: **StartTable** (column spec),
         **RowGroup** (one chunk's data), **EndTable** (close).
         Directives that consume the stream see one row group at a time
         and never materialize the full table."""]

    [body
      """Today the consumer surface for CXCol streams is the CLI and
         the Table API (`cx table info` / `dump` / `load`, and the
         host-language bindings' table methods):"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx table info big.cxbin          [# column spec, row count, size #]
         cx table dump big.cxbin --to=cx  [# stream to CX text            #]"""]

    [body
      """The design direction (not yet shipped as CX-code surface): any
         directive that takes a CXPath iterator participates in a CXCol
         stream. `[?for]` / `[?modify]` /
         `[?map]` / `[?reduce]` (see [[code]]) all participate.
         Bounded memory is preserved end-to-end: a pipeline of N stages
         holds N row groups in flight, not N × row-count."""]

    [child id=streaming-events n=5.6.1 title='Streaming events'
      [body
        """The event vocabulary
           ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
           extends the standard `StartElement` / `Scalar` / `EndElement`
           events with three table-specific variants."""]
      [list
        [item """**StartTable** — fields: `name` (string),
                `col_spec` (binary blob; the column schema in ast_bin
                form). Emitted in place of the `StartElement` that
                would otherwise wrap a `[table[…]]` body."""]
        [item """**RowGroup** — fields: `row_count` (u32), `payload`
                (binary blob; one row group's column buffers). One
                event per row group, in source order."""]
        [item """**EndTable** — fields: `name` (string). Closes the
                sequence in place of the corresponding `EndElement`."""]]
      [body
        """A non-chunked `0x60` (whole-table) input emits the existing
           `StartElement` / per-cell-`Scalar` / `EndElement` sequence
           unchanged. Only `0x63` chunked-CXCol inputs produce the new
           events. Streaming consumers that don't care about the
           distinction can flatten via a helper that re-emits each
           `RowGroup` as a sequence of `StartElement` / row /
           `EndElement` triples."""]]

    [child id=streaming-perf n=5.6.2 title='Performance posture'
      [body
        """The gate protocol is fixed by
           [`spec/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
           §11.4.4: every measured streaming shape must sustain a mean
           ≥ 200 MB/s with no single trial below 80% of the mean, and
           must demonstrably stream (more than one output chunk, the
           implementation's own streaming predicate reporting true).
           Re-run with `make bench-code-streaming`."""]
      [note
        """Current status, honestly stated: the gate is **not green** —
           `[?for]` measures ~200 MB/s and `[?map]` 129.2 MB/s.
           Closing the `[?map]` gap is tracked for the next cycle.
           On the write side, the ABI streaming writer compresses a row
           group only when its body exceeds 64 KiB and compression
           beats 0.85 of the original size
           ([`spec/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.10)."""]]]

  [child id=integrations n=5.7 title='Ecosystem integrations'
    [intro
      """The Arrow C Data Interface is the integration layer the
         analytical ecosystem standardized on. Once CX exposes Arrow,
         the rest of the stack lights up — DuckDB, Polars, DataFusion,
         pandas, Spark via Parquet — most with zero-copy handoff in
         process, the rest with one copy at the boundary."""]

    [child id=duckdb n=5.7.1 title='DuckDB'
      [body
        """DuckDB consumes Arrow tables registered on a connection —
           zero-copy from CXCol's column buffers into DuckDB's
           vectorized executor via the C Data Interface."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib, duckdb
           import cxlib.arrow as cxa

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())
           orders = cxa.export(framed).read_all()   # pyarrow.Table
           con = duckdb.connect()

           # Register the Arrow table — zero-copy via the C Data Interface
           con.register('orders', orders)

           result = con.execute('''
               SELECT customer, SUM(amount) AS total
               FROM orders
               GROUP BY customer
               ORDER BY total DESC
           ''').fetchall()"""]
      [body
        """DuckDB's executor reads directly from CX's column buffers
           during the scan. The result set is materialized into
           DuckDB's own memory; `cxa.import_to_data_bin(con.execute(…)
           .arrow())` brings a result back as framed CXCol bytes — the
           symmetric inverse."""]]

    [child id=polars n=5.7.2 title='Polars'
      [body
        """Polars is Arrow-native at the buffer level. CX → Polars in
           Python goes through the same export call — zero-copy for the
           underlying buffers."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib, polars as pl
           import cxlib.arrow as cxa

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())
           df = pl.from_arrow(cxa.export(framed).read_all())

           # Polars query
           result = df.lazy().filter(pl.col('amount') > 100).collect()"""]
      [body
        """In Rust, the same handoff runs over the C ABI — the
           `cx_arrow_export_open` stream feeds the `arrow` crate's
           `ffi_stream` reader, and Polars consumes the record batches
           from there. A packaged Rust convenience wrapper has not
           shipped."""]]

    [child id=pandas n=5.7.3 title='pandas'
      [body
        """pandas is **not** zero-copy at the buffer level — its column
           model uses NumPy arrays with type-system mismatches against
           Arrow (no nullable integers, no first-class strings without
           StringDtype). CX → pandas is one Arrow round-trip plus one
           pandas conversion; expect one full data copy at the
           boundary."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.arrow as cxa

           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())

           # PyArrow → pandas; one copy at the pandas boundary
           df = cxa.export(framed).read_all().to_pandas()"""]
      [body
        """[`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md) §3
           declares a `to_pandas()` adapter slot on the Table type; the
           Python binding has not implemented it yet — the explicit
           Arrow route above is the shipped path."""]
      [note
        """For DataFrame-style work where zero-copy matters, prefer
           Polars over pandas. pandas remains supported because the
           Python data-science ecosystem assumes it, but the
           cross-boundary cost is real on large tables."""]]

    [child id=datafusion n=5.7.4 title='DataFusion (planned)'
      [body
        """Apache DataFusion is a Rust query engine over Arrow. Its
           `TableProvider` trait accepts arbitrary Arrow record-batch
           sources, so CXCol is a natural fit: register a `.cxbin` file
           as a DataFusion table, run SQL or DataFrame queries against
           it with the same zero-copy guarantees as Polars."""]
      [body
        """Status: **planned** — no packaged CX-side convenience layer
           ships. Adopters who need DataFusion today wire it manually:
           `cx_arrow_export_open` over the C ABI into the `arrow`
           crate's `ffi_stream` reader, and the resulting record
           batches into a DataFusion `MemTable`. The path works; it
           just lacks the convenience layer."""]]

    [child id=spark n=5.7.5 title='Spark and cluster compute'
      [body
        """Spark reads / writes Parquet natively. CXCol's Parquet bridge
           (see [[parquet-bridge]]) is the integration path — emit
           `.parquet` from CX, point Spark at the directory, run
           PySpark / Spark SQL. For zero-copy in cluster contexts,
           Arrow Flight is the long-term path; status is
           **planned** per [[arrow-flight]]."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           import cxlib.parquet as cxpq
           from pyspark.sql import SparkSession

           # CX table → Parquet (one-time conversion)
           framed = cxlib.to_data_bin_chunked(open('orders.cx').read())
           cxpq.write_table(framed, 'orders.parquet')

           # Spark reads the Parquet directly
           spark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()
           df = spark.read.parquet('orders.parquet')
           df.groupBy('customer').sum('amount').show()"""]
      [body
        """The Parquet bridge is one-way at this layer — Spark's writes
           come back through `cx table load output.parquet --to=cx`.
           For round-trip workflows, keep the CXCol file as the
           canonical origin and treat Spark's outputs as derived
           artifacts."""]]]

  [child id=performance n=5.8 title='Performance characteristics'
    [intro
      """The analytics lane's perf budgets and where they're measured.
         The spec fixes the gates; the bench targets re-measure them —
         numbers live where they are re-run, not in prose that goes
         stale."""]

    [list
      [item """**Streaming throughput.** Spec gate: mean ≥ 200 MB/s
              per streaming shape, no trial below 80% of the mean
              ([`spec/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
              §11.4.4, gate 15; bench: `make bench-code-streaming`).
              Current status: not green — `[?for]` ~200 MB/s, `[?map]`
              129.2 MB/s measured; tracked for the next cycle."""]
      [item """**Parquet write (default Snappy, 2²⁰ row-group).**
              Measured via PyArrow's writer on the CXCol → Arrow → Parquet
              path; throughput is dominated by the Parquet codec, not
              the CXCol bridge. Adopters should profile against their
              chosen Parquet writer; no normative CX-side gate."""]
      [item """**Arrow zero-copy handoff.** O(metadata) per batch —
              the schema and per-column buffer pointers are populated,
              no buffer is copied. Time per export is independent
              of row count and proportional to column count."""]
      [item """**Predicate pushdown reduction.** When the Parquet
              writer emitted per-row-group statistics, the host Parquet
              reader skips the row groups a predicate rules out — I/O
              saved is proportional to the groups skipped. Files
              without statistics degrade to a full scan."""]
      [item """**Memory budget per stream stage.** One row group in
              flight. The streaming evaluator
              ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
              is bounded by `max(row_group_byte_size)` regardless of
              table size."""]]

    [note
      """The 200 MB/s gate is for the **streaming evaluator**, not the
         raw CXCol reader — the reader has no evaluator pipeline in
         front of it, but its throughput is not separately gated or
         published. Per-binding adapter throughput varies by host —
         pandas is the slowest because of the boundary copy."""]]

  [child id=cxcol-vs-others n=5.9 title='When to use CXCol vs Parquet vs Arrow vs JSON'
    [intro
      """Pick by use case. The four formats overlap in the analytics
         lane but each has a niche the others fill badly."""]

    [list
      [item """**CXCol** — use when CX content-addressable identity
              matters (caching, dedup, signing, provenance) or when
              you want streaming with the CX type system end to end.
              Single-writer, sequential-decode, hashable. Single-file
              format; not designed for query (use the Arrow bridge into
              DuckDB / Polars / DataFusion for that)."""]
      [item """**Parquet** — use for cross-ecosystem interop (Spark,
              BigQuery, Snowflake, Trino, Athena, all the Hadoop-
              lineage tools). Carries the data, not CX's schema
              identity or metadata (see [[parquet-metadata]]).
              Not hashable in the byte-stable sense — different writers
              produce different bytes for the same logical data."""]
      [item """**Arrow IPC stream** — use for in-process or LAN-local
              zero-copy between CX and another Arrow-native consumer
              (DuckDB, Polars, DataFusion, pandas via PyArrow). No
              compression by default (Arrow is layout-oriented, not
              compression-oriented); not designed for long-term
              storage."""]
      [item """**JSON / JSONL** — use for ad-hoc human-readable interop
              or when the consumer truly is JSON-shaped. Lossy on type
              metadata (no distinction between int and float kinds,
              date-times become strings, dictionary encoding gone). The
              CX → JSON projection (see [[surfaces#json]]) handles the
              shape conversion; for tabular data specifically, JSONL
              (one row per line) is the conventional wire form."""]]

    [body
      """The decision tree, compressed:"""]

    [list
      [item """Need a hashable, content-addressable origin? → **CXCol**."""]
      [item """Sharing with Spark / Snowflake / BigQuery / other
              non-Arrow-native tools? → **Parquet**."""]
      [item """In-process handoff to DuckDB / Polars / DataFusion /
              pandas? → **Arrow** (with CXCol as the origin)."""]
      [item """Debugging, ad-hoc inspection, or a JSON-shaped consumer? →
              **JSON / JSONL**."""]]

    [body
      """In practice the layering is: **CXCol as canonical origin →
         Arrow as the in-process bridge → Parquet for cross-ecosystem
         export**. JSON is the human-readable view. The same logical
         table can move through all four; identity stays at CXCol."""]]

  [child id=see-also n=5.10 title='See also'
    [list
      [item """[[identity]] — canonical bytes + SHA-256. CXCol identity
              rests on the same SHA-256 primitive over canonical bytes;
              the rule extends naturally to chunked tables and
              schema-driven encoding."""]
      [item """[[surfaces]] — the JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown
              projections. CXCol is the analytical surface; the others
              are the interchange surfaces."""]
      [item """[[bindings]] — Layer 1 / Layer 2 host idioms for reading
              and writing CXCol in V / Python / Go / Rust. The Table
              API and the streaming reader / writer live there."""]
      [item """[[code#streaming]] — the directive-level streaming view:
              `[?for]` / `[?modify]` over CXCol inputs in bounded
              memory."""]
      [item """[`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md) — the
              typed columnar Table API ratified across the four
              bindings."""]
      [item """[`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.10-3.13 —
              chunked-table wire format, page-compression wrapper,
              schema-driven mode."""]
      [item """[`spec/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md) — streaming
              evaluator design and the StartTable / RowGroup / EndTable
              event vocabulary."""]]]]

[# End of section 5 — analytics #]
